[1.3149, "o", "\r\n"]
[1.3438, "o", "\r\n  Each census prints a population. Each row below counts the same\r\n  population a different way and subtracts. A counter sharing the\r\n  census's own reader would agree by construction.\r\n\r\n      census                   printed  counted here   population\r\n"]
[13.3799, "o", "      unparsed report             1269          1269   .t27 files\r\n"]
[13.8277, "o", "      quantifiers report           888           888   lines carrying a quantifier keyword\r\n"]
[13.9072, "o", "      mods orphan                  155           155   .rs under every workspace member\r\n"]
[13.929, "o", "      lean vacuous                 250           250   `theorem` lines in Completeness.lean\r\n\r\n  AGREED. Every census speaks about the population it prints.\r\n\r\n  Not checked here, and why -- because a page of green rows looks\r\n  like coverage until somebody asks what is missing from it:\r\n\r\n      seals hollow\r\n          built and removed. Its counter tested json text for `\"spec_path\"`\r\n          while the census parses that same field, so planting one more seal\r\n          moved BOTH numbers to 1314 and the row stayed green. No input makes\r\n          them disagree.\r\n\r\n      types dup\r\n          measured: a counter loose enough to be independent reads 1182 where\r\n          the census reads 1180, and the two extra are `struct = 21,` -- enum\r\n          members named `struct`, which the census correctly rejects. Any\r\n          counter accurate enough to agree is a copy of its matcher.\r\n\r\n      discard classify\r\n          its population is parser events produced at run time, not artefacts\r\n          on disk. Countin"]
[13.9291, "o", "g them a second way means running the same parser,\r\n"]
[13.9291, "o", "          which is not a second opinion.\r\n"]
[13.9297, "x", "0"]
